<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regenten</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as regents</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The regenten were the ruling elite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> from the 16th to the 18th century: the leaders of its cities and the heads of institutions such as orphanages. Not formally a hereditary class, they were in practice patricians, drawn from the rich merchant families who had run the cities since the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Co-opted for life into the town councils they nominated the burgomasters and voted for their cities in the provincial states, and from 1588 they were the republic's rulers in fact and law. Contracts of correspondence later reserved offices to allied families, and only upheavals of 1672, 1747 and 1785 dislodged them, until the revolution of 1795 ended their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regenten.jpg" alt="Regenten" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Hals · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Hals_-_De_regenten_van_het_oudemannenhuis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
